{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"athletic-esthetical-polecat","creation_channel":"python","description":"## Context / Problem\nIn ATTESTED mode — the PRODUCTION default (`source=attested`, `ref=origin/main`) — a scoped\nplan-review attestation is never invalidated by a change to its own reviewed dependency\nfiles. The claim gate's drift check cannot fire, by construction.\n\nMechanism (`src/rebar/llm/plan_review/manifest.py:190-225`, `_hash_basis`): the claim gate\nre-hashes the signed dependency paths at the signature's PINNED `verified_at_sha`:\n\n    * ``pinned_sha`` given (the claim gate, reading the signature's ``verified_at_sha``) ->\n      the materialized snapshot at that SHA\n\nThe manifest's hashes were produced from that same pinned snapshot. Re-hashing the same\nimmutable tree therefore ALWAYS matches, so `drift` is structurally unreachable. Scoping then\nsuppresses the whole-HEAD staleness fallback (that fallback only applies to an EMPTY dependency\nset), so nothing else catches it either — the claim is simply allowed.\n\nNet effect in production: review a plan scoped to `foo.py`, let `foo.py` change on `main`,\nand the attestation still satisfies the claim gate. The gate's dependency-drift protection is\ninert exactly where it was supposed to matter.\n\n## Why it was invisible\n`tests/conftest.py` defaulted the whole offline suite to `source=local`, where the basis is\nthe MUTABLE working tree, so both sides of the comparison move and drift fires normally. Every\ndrift test passes against local-mode semantics that production never uses.\n\nDemonstrated by flipping that default to `attested`/`ref=HEAD` (branch\n`5128-local-never-signs`): five drift tests fail with the attestation NOT invalidating —\n`test_code_drift_in_dependency_file_invalidates` reaches `DID NOT RAISE RebarError`, i.e. the\nclaim SUCCEEDS after the reviewed file changed and was committed.\n\nDiscovered while implementing melancholy-firstborn-shihtzu (5128-0856-d80f-4eb6).\n\n## Complication — a CONFLICTING design authority for 5128\n`manifest.py:206-207` states:\n\n    BACK-OUT: a plan-review signed in local mode (or pre-S4b) carries no ``verified_at_sha``;\n    both sides then resolve to the working tree exactly as before this consolidation.\n\nThat sentence ASSUMES local-mode-signed plan reviews exist and are supported — which\ncontradicts ADR 0005 / `repo_snapshot.SOURCE_LOCAL` / the CLI help (\"local ... never signs\"),\nthe authorities 5128's fix rests on. I did not have this citation when I classified 5128 as an\nunambiguous `defect`; it points the other way and must be reconciled before 5128 lands.\n\nThere is also an order-of-work dependency: the working tree is the ONLY mutable basis, so\nonce local-source signing is removed, the mutable-basis path this drift machinery needs is\nunreachable through any supported route. Fixing 5128 first would leave the drift subsystem\nwith no reachable test basis at all.\n\n## Expected Behavior\nA scoped attestation must be invalidated when a file in its signed dependency set changes in\nthe attested basis (i.e. the current gate ref moved and one of those files differs between the\npinned SHA and the current ref), while an UNRELATED change must still not invalidate it (the\nworm-folly-barge property that scoping exists to preserve).\n\n## Approach\n1. Decide the comparison basis: the drift check should re-hash the dependency set at the\n   CURRENT gate ref and compare against the manifest's pinned hashes — not re-hash at the\n   pinned SHA, which is a tautology.\n2. Preserve the ADR 0002 anti-false-positive property: only the SIGNED dependency paths\n   participate, so unrelated commits still do not invalidate.\n3. Re-point the drift tests at attested semantics (commit the drift; the suite default move\n   is prepared on branch `5128-local-never-signs`).\n4. Reconcile the `manifest.py:206-207` back-out sentence with ADR 0005 and record which wins.\n\n## Scope\n- `src/rebar/llm/plan_review/manifest.py`\n- `src/rebar/llm/plan_review/attest.py`\n- `tests/interfaces/lifecycle/test_plan_review_gate.py`\n- `tests/conftest.py`\n- `docs/plan-review-gate.md`, ADR 0002 / ADR 0005 reconciliation\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] With the suite on the attested default, changing a SIGNED dependency file and committing\n      it invalidates the attestation: `rebar claim` is blocked and the reason names drift.\n- [ ] An UNRELATED committed change still does NOT invalidate a scoped attestation\n      (`test_unrelated_change_does_not_invalidate_attestation` stays green).\n- [ ] Deleting a signed dependency file invalidates.\n- [ ] The drift-refresh path (immaterial drift -> refresh without full re-review) works under\n      attested semantics.\n- [ ] `manifest.py`'s local-mode back-out comment matches whatever is decided for\n      melancholy-firstborn-shihtzu; the two no longer contradict.\n- [ ] `make lint`, `make typecheck`, and the default non-integration pytest suite exit zero.\n","id":"72d9-8fbd-3a9e-48a6","parent_id":"","priority":1,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"bug","title":"Scoped plan-review attestations never detect dependency drift in attested mode (production default)"},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1784945528927768001,"uuid":"095758c5-f635-49b9-bedd-663d94969468"}